  "method_summary": "Landsat Collection 2 Level-2 composites; 4-class Random Forest (200 trees) in Google Earth Engine at 30 m, trained on stable GLC-FCS30D labels; land surface temperature from the Landsat thermal band (April–June primary, November–February secondary); UHI as urban core minus an equal-area rural ring."
 },
 "findings": [
  "Built-up land (including bare land) in Noida–Ghaziabad grew from 616 km² (26.4 %) in 2015 to 669 km² (28.7 %) in 2025, a net +53 km² (+9 %), while GHSL population rose +20 % (6.07 → 7.26 million; the 2025 figure is a GHSL projection).",
  "Cropland was the main source of new built-up land: 155 km² changed from cropland to built-up, but 95 km² was mapped the other way (mostly class confusion), leaving a net conversion of 59 km² and a net cropland change of -73 km².",
  "Vegetation/tree cover did not decline: it changed by +27 km² (139 → 167 km²), so urban growth in this period replaced cropland rather than vegetation and the expected vegetation-loss step is not supported.",
  "In the pre-monsoon season (April–June, ~10:30 overpass) the urban core was -0.55 °C relative to its rural ring in 2015 (cool island) and +0.50 °C in 2025 (heat island), small values whose change of sign cannot be separated from weather with one composite per year, whereas November–February showed a consistent heat island (+1.06 °C and +1.09 °C).",
  "Cropland that became built-up between 2015 and 2025 changed by -0.01 SD in normalised April–June LST, against -0.11 SD for cropland that stayed cropland, a difference of +0.10 SD (+0.06 °C raw), i.e. slightly warmer than the unchanged class; conversion to built-up did not produce a clear summer temperature change."
 ],
 "limitations": [
  {
   "title": "Accuracy is agreement with reference labels, not true accuracy.",
   "text": "Training and test points come from the same GLC-FCS30D-derived labels, restricted to temporally stable pixels (easier than changing ones) and spatially clustered; the figures are optimistic. Independent, stratified validation points (outputs/validation, 50 per class per year) still have to be interpreted on high-resolution imagery."
  },
  {
   "title": "Built-up includes bare land.",
   "text": "Barren/Open land could not be trained (86 and 14 label pixels) and was merged into Built-up in every year; construction sites and cleared plots therefore count as built-up."
  },
  {
   "title": "Few labels for Vegetation/Trees and Water",
   "text": "(2015: 1,652 and 1,434 label pixels; 2025: 1,625 and 2,786 label pixels), from a limited number of patches; their mapped areas are the least certain."
  },
  {
   "title": "Change maps add the errors of two maps",
   "text": "; small transitions (a few km²) are within classification noise."
  },
  {
   "title": "One overpass time.",
   "text": "Landsat sees ~10:30 local time only; night-time UHI (usually the strongest) is not measured."
  },
  {
   "title": "One composite per season per year.",
   "text": "Year-to-year weather differences affect absolute LST; UHI intensity and normalised LST reduce but do not remove this."
  },
  {
   "title": "Thermal resolution.",
   "text": "LST is 100 m (Landsat 8/9) or 60 m (Landsat 7) resampled to 30 m; small features are smoothed."
  },
  {
   "title": "Rural ring inside the study area.",
   "text": "East Delhi (urban) borders the study area to the west, so the ring is restricted to the districts; its location differs between years with the urban core."
  },
  {
   "title": "GHSL 2025 is a projection",
   "text": ", and GHSL population is itself modelled from census and built-up data (not a count)."
  },
  {
   "title": "2005 (once added): Landsat 7 SLC-off",
   "text": "— about 22 % of each scene is missing in stripes; three pooled years fill most gaps, the remainder is reported. No independent label product exists for 2005 (stability filter only), and the sensor differs from 2015/2025 (ETM+ vs OLI)."
  }
 ],
